Greece is in trouble  headlines about the nation's financial  troubles have been flying since 2010  across the Atlantic Puerto Rico is  suffering rising debt and a shrinking  economy with a credit rating of triple C  plus the island is sliding toward a  possible default Puerto Rico is a  territory of the United States and  people are beginning to ask is it  America's Greece the short answer is now  Greece has a population of 11 million  owing 360 billion dollars the country  has a relatively large economic  footprint Puerto Rico on the other hand  has a population of only 3.5 million an  outstanding debt of 73 billion it's a  much smaller player in the world economy  what really matters is who is holding  Greece's debt its eurozone neighbors  including Italy France and Germany have  been propping Greece up if Greece  defaults on its loan payments it could  rock the economic stability of countries  across the eurozone and could cause  Greece to leave the euro in Puerto  Rico's case it's mostly hedge funds and  distressed debt buyers who hold the  islands debt and if the territory  defaults they'll lose money the United  States government has much less of a  reason to get financially involved in  Puerto Rico than the eurozone countries  do in Greece there is little likelihood  Puerto Rico would leave the dollar  therefore little likelihood of a u.s.  bailout
 

